EXTRA QUESTIONS
---------------

Q1. What is fold change?
Ans. Fold change is the ratio of expression in one condition to expression in
     another condition, commonly Treated divided by Control.

Q2. What is log2 fold change?
Ans. It is the base-2 logarithm of the fold change. Positive values generally
     indicate increased expression, while negative values indicate decreased
     expression, assuming Treated/Control is used.

Q3. Why are biological replicates important in RNA-seq?
Ans. Biological replicates help estimate natural biological variation and make
     statistical conclusions more reliable.

Q4. What is normalization in RNA-seq?
Ans. Normalization adjusts for differences such as sequencing depth and
     composition so that expression measurements can be compared more fairly.

Q5. What is a p-value?
Ans. A p-value measures how compatible the observed data are with a specified
     statistical null hypothesis.

Q6. What is an adjusted p-value?
Ans. It is a p-value corrected for multiple hypothesis testing, helping control
     false discoveries when many genes are tested.

Q7. Name commonly used tools for RNA-seq differential expression.
Ans. DESeq2, edgeR, and limma-voom are commonly used tools.

Q8. Can this program identify statistically significant genes?
Ans. No. It identifies only genes with positive or negative raw differences.
     Statistical testing and adjusted p-values are needed to identify
     statistically significant differential expression.
